## Disclosures
- **Forbidden paper.** The task forbids searching for, reading or using the already-published paper on this question (or pages that cite, discuss or reproduce it). We did not look for it, did not read starter-literature item 8 (Huang et al. 2025; cited only as listed), and did not guess its title, authors or results. **Incidental encounters.** This role encountered none. `idea-repo` (idea-repo#6) reports that, while searching for related work, it received truncated (about 500 characters) OpenAlex abstract snippets of two April 2026 preprints on perturbation of Bradley-Terry / maximum-likelihood pairwise rankings: "Perturbation Sensitivity of Maximum-Likelihood Pairwise Ranking in Computational Decision Systems" (arXiv 2604.17805) and "Ranking Abuse via Strategic Pairwise Data Perturbations" (Research Square rs-9459163). It cannot tell whether either is the forbidden paper or cites it; it stopped, did not open or cite either, and passed nothing from them on. Nothing in `code/` or `results/` uses these two items, and no method, number or claim of this study was taken from them. The analysis design (alteration families, exact-refit k*, same-graph null, equivalence margin) was fixed in PREANALYSIS rev 1-2 from the starter literature and from our own derivations.
- **Timing of the analysis plan.** The decision rules (`PREANALYSIS.md`) were written after the code and the synthetic-BT calibration and while the core batch was already running. Before writing them the analyst had seen MT-Bench-human (6 models) and ~28 Arena-55k pairs (debugging the search), and, when rev 2 was written, summaries of Arena-55k and MT-Bench core output. No margin was tuned on a real-data summary; the margins come from the synthetic runs. The note was revised once (rev 2, after a review by `idea-review-repo`). Any rule changed after real results is listed as a deviation in the paper.
- **Bug fixed during the run.** The first `Prepared` kept cluster ids with gaps for the Arena units (max id 51 733, 44 256 distinct), so the cluster bootstrap drew empty clusters. Fixed with compact ids; effect on `se_boot` is +0.5 % (median over pairs, Arena single-turn); every unit reported here was (re)computed with the fixed `Prepared` (the full core batch was rerun after the fix), so no refreshed-in-place columns remain. `01b_refresh_boot.py` is kept for the case that `Prepared` changes again.
- **What k\* is.** k\* is the smallest alteration count found by the search whose exact BT refit reverses the pair: a verified **upper bound** on the minimum. The cost-2 (full-win flip) k\* is also an upper bound found by search. The LP-relaxation value is a local multi-start candidate for a lower bound and is **not certified**; it is reported as a ratio to the verified upper bound. Exhaustive search agrees with the iterated search only on tiny problems (53 of 53).
- **Not run.** Insertion family; Arena-140k and prompt slices; WebDev Arena; Rao-Kupper / Davidson models; Elo; NBA home-advantage sensitivity. Equal-N scaling (`05_scaling.py`) was run on ten units (Arena-55k, Arena single-turn, MT-Bench human, MT-Bench GPT-4, three LLM judges, NBA 1990 / 2005 / 2015), not on all 91. CI and random-baseline files exist for every unit used in the survival table.
- **Survival table.** An earlier version of `survival_curves.csv` silently held only part of the units (file-name mismatch `judge:X` vs `judge_X`); fixed. The delivered file keeps `sep_def` = published, `subset` = separated and axes `frac`, `se` (repository size limit); `10_tables_more.py` writes the full grid to `$BT_RESULTS/tables_full/survival_curves_full.csv`.
- **Confounding.** Designs are not crossed with data sets (one expert data set, two crowd data sets, 18 LLM-judge sets of the same battles, 69 NBA seasons); design effects are estimated by the matched contrasts and by leave-one-data-set-out / leave-one-design-out regressions, not by a randomised comparison.
- **Positive control is synthetic.** `scripts/14_positive_control.py` simulates outcomes from the BT model fitted to the real design (Arena-55k; MT-Bench human) with one departure added (overdispersion, position bias, cyclic component, pair-specific deviation) at four fixed sizes (`PREANALYSIS.md` rev 3), and applies the Δ(log Q) pipeline to the simulated data. It shows what the statistic can detect on those two graphs; it does not show that a real data set has or lacks such a departure. Arena-55k has no question or battle cluster for resampling, so comparisons are cut into random blocks of 20 as the resampling unit (`rng.permutation(n) // 20`) (an assumption, not a recorded grouping). Sizes are on the logit scale and were fixed in `PREANALYSIS.md` rev 3 to span from a small departure to one large enough to find where the test breaks (overdispersion sd 2 gives a design effect of about 6; position bias 2 shifts a win probability by up to 0.4); they are not calibrated to departures measured in real data, so "not detected" refers to these sizes only. Replicates: 5 (Arena; null 8) and 20 (MT-Bench; null 20); each uses a sample of 300 pairs spread over z. "Detected" means the replicate-mean interval lies beyond the +-0.15 margin.
- **SE ratio.** `scripts/13_se_ratio.py`: se_boot/se_fisher, from the cluster bootstrap and from a comparison-level bootstrap, for the real data and for 4 same-graph parametric-bootstrap null replicates (B=200 each). The model-based SE ignores that ties carry less variance, so ratios below 1 in tie-heavy data are expected and appear in the null as well; read the real/null ratio, not the raw ratio.
- **Predictive-rule references.** The first-order and null-mean reference predictors exist only for units that have those columns; `mtbench_gpt4` has neither and is left out of the held-out comparison (89 units for the design comparison, 90 for all units).
- **LLM use.** The code, the analysis and this text were produced by an AI agent (Claude Sonnet 5.5) working as the analysis role of a multi-role research group; every number in the delivered tables comes from a script in `code/`.
